WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.247 --> 00:00:02.497
- When I had my accident...

00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:06.490
I found out, at that moment,

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.728
nothin' in life is promised, except death.

00:00:08.728 --> 00:00:11.542
When someone comes up and says
somethin' like, I am a god,

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:13.781
everybody says, who does he think he is?

00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:17.473
I just told you who I
thought I was, a god!

00:00:17.473 --> 00:00:18.553
I just told you!

00:00:18.553 --> 00:00:19.763
That's who I think I am!

00:00:19.763 --> 00:00:23.412
To this date, they do
not understand who I am.

00:00:23.412 --> 00:00:26.973
They will not understand,
'til after I'm gone.

00:00:26.973 --> 00:00:29.873
- He's an American rapper,
songwriter, record producer,

00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:31.783
fashion designer, and entrepreneur.

00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.955
He's among the most acclaimed
musicians of the 21st century,

00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:37.622
and is one of the bestselling
artists of all time.

00:00:37.622 --> 00:00:39.091
He's also been included in a number

00:00:39.091 --> 00:00:41.132
of the Forbes annual lists.

00:00:41.132 --> 00:00:42.121
He's Kanye West,

00:00:42.121 --> 00:00:44.661
and here's my take on his
top ten rules for success,

00:00:44.661 --> 00:00:45.676
volume two.

00:00:45.676 --> 00:00:46.547
And as always, guys,

00:00:46.547 --> 00:00:48.744
if you hear somethin' that
really resonates with you,

00:00:48.744 --> 00:00:50.486
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:50.486 --> 00:00:51.609
Put quotes around it as well,

00:00:51.609 --> 00:00:53.336
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:53.336 --> 00:00:54.636
Also, when you write it down,

00:00:54.636 --> 00:00:57.733
you're much more likely to lock
it in for yourself as well.

00:00:57.733 --> 00:00:58.566
Enjoy.

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:04.526
(inspirational music)

00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:12.832
- Man, I just had the hunger.

00:01:12.832 --> 00:01:14.332
Like, my mother...

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.107
Never raised me to ask for a handout.

00:01:19.107 --> 00:01:21.038
Like, a lot of people
that got into this game,

00:01:21.038 --> 00:01:23.626
they walkin' around with
somebody else's chain on,

00:01:23.626 --> 00:01:25.145
or drivin' somebody else's car,

00:01:25.145 --> 00:01:26.439
and didn't even pay for the shoes

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:27.861
that they got on their feet.

00:01:27.861 --> 00:01:30.122
My mother had a decent job.

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:30.955
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:01:30.955 --> 00:01:34.691
But, she gave me a little
allowance, 15, 20 dollars a week,

00:01:34.691 --> 00:01:37.291
and I had to go out and
get my own job at age 15.

00:01:37.291 --> 00:01:39.790
Matter of fact, at 14, I was
cuttin' hair in a barbershop.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.427
So, it was instilled in me,
to go out, and just get it.

00:01:43.427 --> 00:01:46.376
And that, that made me be a leader,

00:01:46.376 --> 00:01:48.105
around a lot of black men.

00:01:48.105 --> 00:01:48.994
Like, even with my father.

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:50.863
I had two fathers in my life.

00:01:50.863 --> 00:01:52.976
I had my real father, that
I was in contact with,

00:01:52.976 --> 00:01:55.347
and my stepfather, that
stayed there with my mother,

00:01:55.347 --> 00:01:58.308
and everybody was instillin'
that responsibility in me,

00:01:58.308 --> 00:02:00.479
that nothin' in life is given to you.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:02.545
A lot of times, I feel like
a lot of people just rappin',

00:02:02.545 --> 00:02:03.840
'cause it's free.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:04.673
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:02:04.673 --> 00:02:06.078
What you got to do, especially now,

00:02:06.078 --> 00:02:07.468
with Jay writin' his rhymes in his head.

00:02:07.468 --> 00:02:09.448
People ain't got to pay for paper no more.

00:02:09.448 --> 00:02:11.378
Everybody just feel
like they feelin' just,

00:02:11.378 --> 00:02:12.211
you know what I'm sayin',

00:02:12.211 --> 00:02:13.917
come out and be a rapper out the blue.

00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:15.180
At least with a production,

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.866
you have to go up and figure out a way

00:02:16.866 --> 00:02:21.008
to get your equipment
up, and go buy records,

00:02:21.008 --> 00:02:22.239
and do different things.

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:26.224
So, I'm talkin' about the
work ethic is so serious.

00:02:26.224 --> 00:02:28.453
And up here, at Bassline, up at the Roc,

00:02:28.453 --> 00:02:30.836
people work, the work ethic is crazy!

00:02:30.836 --> 00:02:32.561
That's why I feel like

00:02:32.561 --> 00:02:34.296
we re-killin' the game right now.

00:02:34.296 --> 00:02:35.204
That's why we cuttin' edge.

00:02:35.204 --> 00:02:38.263
That's why we on flex, every
week, just crushin' labels.

00:02:38.263 --> 00:02:40.759
That's why all you hear,
practically, is the Roc.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:42.842
When I had my accident...

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.040
I found out at that moment,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.540
nothin' in life is promised, except death.

00:02:52.602 --> 00:02:57.478
If you have the opportunity
to play this game of life,

00:02:57.478 --> 00:03:00.978
you need to appreciate every moment.

00:03:00.978 --> 00:03:03.312
A lot of people don't
appreciate their moment,

00:03:03.312 --> 00:03:04.812
until it's passed.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.117
(audience applauding)

00:03:11.967 --> 00:03:13.697
And then, you got to tell
those Al Bundy stories,

00:03:13.697 --> 00:03:16.205
you remember when I...

00:03:16.205 --> 00:03:17.038
But...

00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:22.260
Right now, is my time, and my moment,

00:03:23.655 --> 00:03:26.047
thanks to the fans,
thanks to the accident,

00:03:26.047 --> 00:03:28.784
(audience cheering)
thanks to God,

00:03:28.784 --> 00:03:33.114
thanks to Roc-A-Fella, Jay-Z,
Dame Dash, G, my mother,

00:03:33.114 --> 00:03:36.377
Rhymefest, everyone that's helped me,

00:03:36.377 --> 00:03:38.377
and I plan to celebrate.

00:03:40.156 --> 00:03:43.926
I plan to celebrate, and
scream, and pop champagne,

00:03:43.926 --> 00:03:45.125
every chance I get,
(audience cheering)

00:03:45.125 --> 00:03:47.792
'cause I'm at the Grammys, baby!

00:03:54.964 --> 00:03:56.616
I know, I know every,

00:03:56.616 --> 00:03:58.575
I know everybody asked me the question.

00:03:58.575 --> 00:04:01.907
They wanted to know, I know
he's going to wil' out,

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:04.014
I know he's going to do somethin' crazy!

00:04:04.014 --> 00:04:09.004
Everybody want to know what
I would do, if I didn't win.

00:04:09.004 --> 00:04:11.192
(audience laughing)

00:04:11.192 --> 00:04:15.630
I guess we'll never know.
(audience cheering)

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.797
You don't realize, I am so frustrated.

00:04:19.862 --> 00:04:21.464
Like, I am so frustrated.

00:04:21.464 --> 00:04:24.616
Like, I am so, I've got
so much I want to give.

00:04:24.616 --> 00:04:26.956
I've got ideas on color palettes.

00:04:26.956 --> 00:04:30.105
I've got ideas on silhouettes.

00:04:30.105 --> 00:04:31.594
And I've got a million people tellin' me

00:04:31.594 --> 00:04:33.068
why I can't do it.

00:04:33.068 --> 00:04:34.990
You know, that I'm not a
real designer, I'm not this.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.691
I'm not a real rapper, either!

00:04:36.691 --> 00:04:38.409
I'm not a real musician, either!

00:04:38.409 --> 00:04:40.066
Like, I don't know how to play the piano!

00:04:40.066 --> 00:04:40.914
(Zane chuckling)

00:04:40.914 --> 00:04:41.747
Like, I'm a artist!

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:43.416
I went to art college!

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.446
I went to art college, and
was looked at like I'm soft,

00:04:46.446 --> 00:04:49.476
'cause I like more Italian clothes.

00:04:49.476 --> 00:04:50.309
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:04:50.309 --> 00:04:54.746
When I'm rappin' in front a
camera, and my pants is tight...

00:04:54.746 --> 00:04:57.635
Tryna spit a verse for cam!

00:04:57.635 --> 00:05:00.218
You know, so, that's the
same, when I'm sittin' there,

00:05:00.218 --> 00:05:04.301
you know, like, sittin'
there, at a fashion show.

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:06.037
Like...

00:05:07.277 --> 00:05:09.407
I'm there, because I
appreciate Phoebe Philo.

00:05:09.407 --> 00:05:10.446
I appreciate Raf Simons.

00:05:10.446 --> 00:05:12.228
I appreciate Riccardo Tisci,

00:05:12.228 --> 00:05:14.997
and I look at them as my
peers, as creative people,

00:05:14.997 --> 00:05:19.299
who dedicate their entire life
to making something better

00:05:19.299 --> 00:05:20.465
for the world.

00:05:20.465 --> 00:05:23.092
Let me explain what anybody,
that knows a artist,

00:05:23.092 --> 00:05:24.892
and you know someone that's giving that.

00:05:24.892 --> 00:05:27.780
If you know a artist, there's
only one thing you can say,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.467
give, or ask them, when you see them.

00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:32.032
There are two words.

00:05:32.032 --> 00:05:32.865
Thank you.

00:05:34.682 --> 00:05:36.420
There's no, why didn't you do this?

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.552
Yo, I want that!

00:05:37.552 --> 00:05:38.835
What's up with...

00:05:38.835 --> 00:05:39.776
No!

00:05:39.776 --> 00:05:42.745
We dedicate our entire life

00:05:42.745 --> 00:05:46.863
to making our current time
in civilization better,

00:05:46.863 --> 00:05:49.574
to adding something to the culture.

00:05:49.574 --> 00:05:52.523
And there is only two
words that you can say.

00:05:52.523 --> 00:05:53.435
Thank you.

00:05:53.435 --> 00:05:55.083
- You've kind of equated yourself

00:05:55.083 --> 00:05:56.137
to all these different people.

00:05:56.137 --> 00:05:59.158
You know, da Vinci, Walt Disney,
Steve Jobs, Michelangelo.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:01.065
And he's asked, how would
you define a genius,

00:06:01.065 --> 00:06:01.953
in that context,

00:06:01.953 --> 00:06:03.184
but also, why do you feel the need

00:06:03.184 --> 00:06:04.934
to call yourself that?

00:06:04.934 --> 00:06:07.639
- Because, otherwise,
I'm called celebrity.

00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:09.342
I'm called nigger.

00:06:09.342 --> 00:06:10.842
I'm called rapper.

00:06:13.366 --> 00:06:15.995
And when they use the words
celebrity, nigger, or rapper,

00:06:15.995 --> 00:06:18.515
it's not in a positive way.

00:06:18.515 --> 00:06:19.348
And all these things,

00:06:19.348 --> 00:06:21.452
all these words can be used
in a extremely positive way.

00:06:21.452 --> 00:06:22.601
What's up, my nigga?

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:25.243
You know, but that's
not the way it's used.

00:06:25.243 --> 00:06:27.743
So, I have to define who I am.

00:06:29.071 --> 00:06:31.544
You know, all of my aspirations are things

00:06:31.544 --> 00:06:35.120
that currently only 60
year old white people do.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.787
So, I have to redefine,
and let people know,

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:41.253
exactly who I am.

00:06:41.253 --> 00:06:45.420
And it's not letting them know
by, you know, wearing a suit,

00:06:47.011 --> 00:06:49.098
by letting them know by wearing a Rolex,

00:06:49.098 --> 00:06:51.008
or letting them know by, you know,

00:06:51.008 --> 00:06:54.619
bragging about how much
money a sponsorship made

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:56.110
on top of a rap.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:59.721
It's letting them know by
saying, let's start with this.

00:06:59.721 --> 00:07:01.541
I'm a creative genius.

00:07:01.541 --> 00:07:03.190
(both chuckling)

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.322
And it's proven.
- Yeah.

00:07:04.322 --> 00:07:05.793
- And I will continue to prove it.

00:07:05.793 --> 00:07:08.292
- You know, six albums,
original albums of your own,

00:07:08.292 --> 00:07:11.201
a Throne record, various
records with GOOD Music,

00:07:11.201 --> 00:07:12.214
to talk about, man.

00:07:12.214 --> 00:07:14.606
But seriously, Yeezus, bravo dude.

00:07:14.606 --> 00:07:16.549
I mean, that is one of
the most creative records,

00:07:16.549 --> 00:07:18.170
you know, of any genre I've heard

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:19.259
in a very, very long time.

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:21.589
And, just in terms of your output,

00:07:21.589 --> 00:07:23.749
the most exciting-sounding
record, I think, man.

00:07:23.749 --> 00:07:25.148
- Oh, thank you very much.

00:07:25.148 --> 00:07:28.464
Yeah, I feel, you know,
I was able to start

00:07:28.464 --> 00:07:31.678
just making exactly what
was in my mind, again,

00:07:31.678 --> 00:07:34.711
not havin' to speak with
the textures of the time.

00:07:34.711 --> 00:07:36.688
'Cause you know, Cruel Summer
is definitely Kanye West,

00:07:36.688 --> 00:07:39.678
and it's somethin' kind of
weird and off about Mercy,

00:07:39.678 --> 00:07:41.849
like when it has the
high-pitch, (imitates sound)

00:07:41.849 --> 00:07:42.682
that type of sound,

00:07:42.682 --> 00:07:44.577
and it sounds like art,
still, a little bit,

00:07:44.577 --> 00:07:47.860
even though it's, obviously,
was a radio smash.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.688
But, it still, when I get into the idea,

00:07:51.688 --> 00:07:53.380
like the trap drums, and things like that,

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:57.098
there are certain songs
that are blatant radio hits,

00:07:57.098 --> 00:07:59.320
it's like I'm speaking
with today's textures,

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.070
and that's, you look,

00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:03.974
if you look at it 200 years from now,

00:08:03.974 --> 00:08:05.312
it's not going to stand out,

00:08:05.312 --> 00:08:08.895
in the way that 808s,
or Yeezus stands out,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.333
and completely can push, or redefine,

00:08:12.333 --> 00:08:15.250
or make people say, hey,
I completely hate that,

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.620
or I completely love that,

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.187
but, let me just think differently,

00:08:18.187 --> 00:08:21.409
because everybody is bound to these,

00:08:21.409 --> 00:08:25.576
no pun intended, they're
bound to 16 bars, eight bars,

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.790
and you know, the normal
radio, the radio thing.

00:08:32.061 --> 00:08:33.330
I was talkin' to Frank Ocean about this,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.299
and said, my mom got
arrested for the sit-ins.

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:37.892
And now, we're more like the sit-outs.

00:08:37.892 --> 00:08:39.950
Like, sit-off of radio.
- Yeah.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:42.591
- And say, hey radio, come to us.

00:08:42.591 --> 00:08:43.424
- [Zane] Yeah.

00:08:43.424 --> 00:08:44.850
- You know, we need to find something new,

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:47.819
because it's being controlled in a way,

00:08:47.819 --> 00:08:49.526
and manufactured in a way

00:08:49.526 --> 00:08:52.693
that, you know, really awesome artists

00:08:55.466 --> 00:08:59.469
can make amazing music,
and not break, as far pass.

00:08:59.469 --> 00:09:01.919
It's like somethin' that's very formulaic.

00:09:01.919 --> 00:09:03.571
- So, it always feels like a
duty to you, in a weird way,

00:09:03.571 --> 00:09:05.541
having the people's ear,
having people's attention,

00:09:05.541 --> 00:09:07.538
and for great music, to be able to say,

00:09:07.538 --> 00:09:08.717
well, if I'm not challenging them enough,

00:09:08.717 --> 00:09:10.493
if I'm not challenging
myself, not challenging radio,

00:09:10.493 --> 00:09:11.474
what am I doing?

00:09:11.474 --> 00:09:12.307
- Yeah.

00:09:12.307 --> 00:09:14.185
Well, I'm not tryin'
to regurgitate myself.

00:09:14.185 --> 00:09:18.348
I showed people that I
understand how to make perfect.

00:09:18.348 --> 00:09:20.013
You know, Dark Fantasy could be considered

00:09:20.013 --> 00:09:20.849
to be perfect to you.

00:09:20.849 --> 00:09:22.599
I say, I know how to make perfect.

00:09:22.599 --> 00:09:24.126
But, that's not what I'm here to do.

00:09:24.126 --> 00:09:27.287
I'm here to crack, and crack the pavement,

00:09:27.287 --> 00:09:31.977
and make new grounds, sonically,
in society, culturally.

00:09:31.977 --> 00:09:35.858
Some people say, everyone
was born an artist.

00:09:35.858 --> 00:09:40.638
And, in society, their parents,
they beat it out of them.

00:09:40.638 --> 00:09:41.671
You know, there's times,

00:09:41.671 --> 00:09:42.818
there was someone,

00:09:42.818 --> 00:09:46.568
after I said I was going
to run for president...

00:09:47.546 --> 00:09:49.821
That showed me, like a video tape,

00:09:49.821 --> 00:09:54.169
of somebody makin' fun of
me, about that concept.

00:09:54.169 --> 00:09:56.262
And I was thinkin' like, wow.

00:09:56.262 --> 00:10:00.429
If this person was doing
this from my age of three,

00:10:01.629 --> 00:10:03.546
I would have never been me.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:06.893
And...

00:10:07.953 --> 00:10:12.594
You know, people say, a genius
is kids with good parents.

00:10:12.594 --> 00:10:13.427
So...

00:10:17.421 --> 00:10:22.251
How do you nurture the things
that people would call weird,

00:10:22.251 --> 00:10:23.084
and...

00:10:26.059 --> 00:10:28.582
To something that could
be, considered to be,

00:10:28.582 --> 00:10:31.620
to something that would be exceptional?

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.620
Like, so many movies are based on...

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:42.359
This antihero that's up against all odds,

00:10:42.359 --> 00:10:43.819
doing the exceptional.

00:10:43.819 --> 00:10:45.752
I was always rappin'.

00:10:45.752 --> 00:10:47.993
And it just so happened that really,

00:10:47.993 --> 00:10:50.811
really phenomenal rappers
got to rap on my beats

00:10:50.811 --> 00:10:52.260
before I got a chance to.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:57.110
So, that pushed me into the
classification of a producer.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:58.750
But, I'm a rapper from the heart.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:00.047
Like, I got somethin' to say.

00:11:00.047 --> 00:11:00.880
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.897
And people like, yo,
what you finna rap about?

00:11:02.897 --> 00:11:04.726
You never sold crack out your house,

00:11:04.726 --> 00:11:07.594
or a put a gat to a mouth, and
put your fist to your spouse.

00:11:07.594 --> 00:11:09.356
So, how you gon' move the crowd?

00:11:09.356 --> 00:11:11.773
I bet a thousand that you get booed out.

00:11:11.773 --> 00:11:12.606
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:11:12.606 --> 00:11:14.615
Like now, the rap game
has changed so much,

00:11:14.615 --> 00:11:16.673
it go from Tribe Called Quest, to Onyx,

00:11:16.673 --> 00:11:20.673
to Swizz Beatz, to
Roc-A-Fella, to the Bad Boys.

00:11:22.074 --> 00:11:24.193
So many different sounds,
and there's, you know,

00:11:24.193 --> 00:11:25.685
it's like, almost playing double dutch.

00:11:25.685 --> 00:11:26.826
Like, where do you get in the game?

00:11:26.826 --> 00:11:28.878
